---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Sources from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Sources.astro";
import { u } from '../lib/url.js';
import sources from '../data/sources.json';

/* 21 September 2026. This page was two hand-written lists and nothing else in
   the estate could count it. It is data now — src/data/sources.json — and the
   extraction was checked the only way an extraction can be: every word of
   every entry was compared before and after, and none is missing. The two
   group headings are unchanged, because "Read, and cited" against
   "Identified, not yet worked" is this archive's whole argument about itself.

   Seventeen of the eighteen sources it has actually read now link to the
   thing; the eighteenth is a scan the family holds and says so. The six under
   "Identified, not yet worked" carry nothing, which is what that heading
   means. A hostname that was written in brackets after a title has moved into
   the link and is printed under it; a bracket holding anything else —
   "(Laws.Africa / AfricanLII)", "(public, no account)" — stayed in the prose,
   because that is content and not an address. */
const groups = sources.groups.map((g) => ({ label: g.group, rows: g.rows }));
const read = sources.groups[0].rows;
const reachable = read.filter((r) => r.url || r.held).length;
---
<Base title="Sources"
  subtitle="What has been read, and what has only been identified"
  lede="Named so that every claim on this site can be traced back, and so that the gap between what is actually held and what is merely known to exist stays visible.">

  <p class="small">Of the {read.length} sources this archive has read,
  <strong>{reachable}</strong> now say where they are — the one that carries no link is a scan
  the family holds, and it says so. The six below it are named and not yet reached, which is
  what that heading means.</p>

  <Sources groups={groups} u={u} facets={false} />

  <h2>On the family tree as a source</h2>
  <p>
    It is a good tree and it is not an archive. It carries certified citations alongside family
    estimates, at the same apparent weight, and it contains at least two duplicated people. Three of its
    statements are contradicted here on the evidence: Jacob Leibholz's death year, Georg Leibholz's
    parentage, and the February 1948 marriage date. That is not a complaint. A tree that records what
    the family believed is exactly the right thing to start from, and the corrections were only possible
    because it recorded enough to be checked.
  </p>
</Base>
